.TH GRAPHS.CFG 5 "Version 4.3.30:  4 Sep 2019" "Xymon"
.SH NAME
graphs.cfg \- Configuration of the showgraph CGI

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B $XYMONHOME/etc/graphs.cfg

.SH DESCRIPTION
.I showgraph.cgi(1)
uses the configuration file $XYMONHOME/etc/graphs.cfg to
build graphs from the RRD files collected by Xymon.

.SH FILE FORMAT
Each definition of a graph type begins with a "[SERVICE]" indicator,
this is the name passed as the "service" parameter to 
.I showgraph.cgi(1).
If the service name passed to showgraph.cgi is not found, it will
attempt to match the service name to a graph via the TEST2RRD environment
variable. So calling showgraph.cgi with "service=cpu" or "service=la"
will end up producing the same graph.

A graph definition needs to have a TITLE and a YAXIS setting. These are
texts shown as the title of the graph, and the YAXIS heading respectively.
(The X-axis is always time-based).

If a fixed set of RRD files are used for the graph, you just write those
in the RRDtool definitions. Note that Xymon keeps all RRD files for a host 
in a separate directory per host, so you need not worry about the hostname 
being part of the RRD filename.

For graphs that use multiple RRD files as input, you specify a filename
pattern in the FNPATTERN statement, and optionally a pattern of files
to exclude from the graph with EXFNPATTERN (see "[tcp]" for an example).
When FNPATTERN is used, you can use "@RRDFN@" in the RRDtool definitions
to pick up each filename. "@RRDIDX@" is an index (starting at 0) for each
file in the set. "@RRDPARAM@" contains the first word extracted from the
pattern of files (see e.g. "[memory]" how this is used). "@COLOR@" picks
a new color for each graph automatically.
Aggregate values across all files selected by FNPATTERN can be built with
"@AVG:name@", "@SUM:name@", "@MIN:name@", "@MAX:name@", "@AVGNAN:name@",
"@SUMNAN:name@", "@MINNAN:name@", "@MAXNAN:name@", "@COUNT:name@",
"@MEDIAN:name@", "@STDEV:name@", "@PERCENT:name:pct@" and "@Ppct:name@"
(short form, e.g. "@P95:name@" or "@P99.9:name@"). The "name" portion
is the variable-name prefix used by preceding "DEF:name@RRDIDX@=..."
lines -- the aggregate expands to "name0,name1,...,nameN-1" followed
by the operator's RPN. "pct" is a decimal percentile (0-100, at most
one dot).
For example, after defining "DEF:t@RRDIDX@=@RRDFN@:ds0:AVERAGE", the
line "CDEF:avg=@AVG:t@" expands to "CDEF:avg=t0,t1,+,t2,+,3,/" when
three files are matched; "@P95:t@" expands to the matching RRDtool
PERCENT RPN; "@PERCENT:t:99.9@" is the long form of "@P99.9:t@".

When the surrounding graph is sliced by "first"/"count" CGI parameters,
the aggregates honour the slice: only the selected RRDs (firstidx
through lastidx inclusive) contribute. An empty selection yields
"UNKN" for arithmetic ops and "0" for "@COUNT:" (so dividing a
"@SUMNAN:" by a "@COUNT:" stays safe on the empty set: 0/0 in RRDtool
RPN is UNKN as well).

Semantics that rely on RRDtool's RPN operators (so they match what
"rrdgraph_rpn(1)" describes): "@STDEV:" emits the *population* standard
deviation (divides by N, not N-1). "@MEDIAN:" returns the lower middle
value when N is even. "@AVG:" divides by the selected count, not the
non-NaN count -- if any contributor is UNKN the result is UNKN; for a
NaN-aware mean combine "@SUMNAN:" with "@COUNT:".

Names accept alphanumerics and underscore (and the inner ":" in the
"@PERCENT:name:pct@" form). "@DSIDX@"/"@PREVDSIDX@"-style index tokens
must NOT appear inside an aggregate operand -- they are not expanded
recursively, and the aggregate parser stops at the first "@" when
locating the operand terminator.

For graphs that repeat a similar line per data source within a single
RRD file (e.g. ping1..pingN slots of a SmokePing-style RRD), use the
"DSCOUNT N" directive together with the "@DSIDX@" token. At parse
time, every def line containing "@DSIDX@" is expanded into N copies
with @DSIDX@ replaced by 1..N. The companion "@PREVDSIDX@" token
expands to (current index \- 1); a line that uses it automatically
loops 2..N to avoid producing an invalid "ping0" reference. To
override the loop's lower bound for a specific line, prefix it with
"@DSSTART:K@" \- the prefix is stripped and K becomes the new lower
bound. If DSCOUNT is omitted but @DSIDX@ is used, the count is read
at render time from the actual RRD file, so a block doesn't need to
repeat the count and hosts with different sample counts each get
every band.

For a bundle viewed one service at a time (e.g. "tcp:conn"), FNPATTERN
capture group 1 must be exactly the service component: showgraph uses it
both as the legend (@RRDPARAM@) and to select the single service \- see
"[tcp]", where "^tcp.(.+).rrd" captures "conn" from tcp.conn.rrd.

To draw the total of a dataset across all matched files, define the
per-file DEF and sum it with "@SUMNAN:" (unknown values count as 0;
use "@SUM:" if one silent contributor should make the total unknown
instead). Such a graph should render unsliced \- a paged aggregate
would show partial sums \- so either give the entry no split size or
have the status declare it with count=all (see xymond_rrd(8) on
self-describing statuses). Example \- one curve with the total read
rate across all disks:
.sp
[diskio_ops_total]
.br
        FNPATTERN ^diskio_ops\\.(.+)\\.rrd
.br
        TITLE Total disk operations
.br
        YAXIS Operations/sec
.br
        DEF:t@RRDIDX@=@RRDFN@:reads:AVERAGE
.br
        CDEF:rtotal=@SUMNAN:t@
.br
        LINE2:rtotal#00CC00:total reads
.sp
Alternatively "@STACKIT@" stacks the per-file curves, whose silhouette
is also the sum while keeping each file's contribution visible.

The remainder of the lines in each definition are passed directly to the
RRDtool rrd_graph() routine.

The following is an example of how the "la" (cpu) graph is defined. This
is a simple definition that uses a single RRD-file, la.rrd:
.sp
[la]
.br
        TITLE CPU Load
.br
        YAXIS Load
.br
        DEF:avg=la.rrd:la:AVERAGE
.br
        CDEF:la=avg,100,/
.br
        AREA:la#00CC00:CPU Load Average
.br
        GPRINT:la:LAST: \: %5.1lf (cur)
.br
        GPRINT:la:MAX: \: %5.1lf (max)
.br
        GPRINT:la:MIN: \: %5.1lf (min)
.br
        GPRINT:la:AVERAGE: \: %5.1lf (avg)\en
.sp

Here is an example of a graph that uses multiple RRD-files, determined
automatically at run-time via the FNPATTERN setting. Note how it uses
the @RRDIDX@ to define a unique RRD parameter per input-file, and the
@COLOR@ and @RRDPARAM@ items to pick unique colors and a matching text
for the graph legend:
.sp
[disk]
.br
        FNPATTERN disk(.*).rrd
.br
        TITLE Disk Utilization
.br
        YAXIS % Full
.br
        DEF:p@RRDIDX@=@RRDFN@:pct:AVERAGE
.br
        LINE2:p@RRDIDX@#@COLOR@:@RRDPARAM@
.br
        \-u 100
.br
        \-l 0
.br
        GPRINT:p@RRDIDX@:LAST: \: %5.1lf (cur)
.br
        GPRINT:p@RRDIDX@:MAX: \: %5.1lf (max)
.br
        GPRINT:p@RRDIDX@:MIN: \: %5.1lf (min)
.br
        GPRINT:p@RRDIDX@:AVERAGE: \: %5.1lf (avg)\en

.SH ADVANCED GRAPH  TITLES
Normally the title of a graph is a static text defined in
the graphs.cfg file. However, there may be situations where
you want to use different titles for the same type of graph, e.g.
if you are incorporating RRD files from MRTG into Xymon. In that
case you can setup the TITLE definition so that it runs a custom
script to determine the graph title. Like this:
.sp
	TITLE exec:/usr/local/bin/graphitle
.sp
The \fB/usr/local/bin/graphtitle\fR command is then called with 
the hostname, the graphtype, the period string, and all of the 
RRD files used as parameters. The script must generate one line of output,
which is then used as the title of the graph. Each of the RRD pathname
parameters will be enclosed in double quotes.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.BR TEST2RRD
Maps service names to graph definitions.

.SH NOTES
Most of the RRD graph definitions shipped with Xymon have been ported 
from the definitions in the \fBlarrd\-grapher.cgi\fR CGI from LARRD 0.43c.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonserver.cfg(5), rrdtool(1), rrdgraph(1)

